From: iosvm glwire
Subject: [PATCH] screendump: write a PPM row's pixels, not pixman's padded stride

ppm_save() fills a line buffer of PIXMAN_BE_r8g8b8 and then writes
pixman_image_get_stride(linebuf) bytes of it. Pixman may pad a row (for a
1125-pixel wide image the stride is 3376, not 1125 * 3 = 3375), and P6 has no
padding: a reader that follows the format sees every row start one byte early,
which shears the picture by one pixel per row. On a 2436-row panel that looks
like a diagonal wipe -- in this project it was mistaken for a frame from a
slide-in animation, and a live console surface was called a snapshot because of
it.

Write width * 3 bytes per row.

diff --git a/ui/ui-qmp-cmds.c b/ui/ui-qmp-cmds.c
--- a/ui/ui-qmp-cmds.c
+++ b/ui/ui-qmp-cmds.c
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@ static bool ppm_save(int fd, pixman_image_t *image, Error **errp)
         qemu_pixman_linebuf_fill(linebuf, image, width, 0, y);
         if (qio_channel_write_all(QIO_CHANNEL(ioc),
                                   (char *)pixman_image_get_data(linebuf),
-                                  pixman_image_get_stride(linebuf), errp) < 0) {
+                                  (size_t)width * 3, errp) < 0) {
             return false;
         }
     }
